meta {
  name: order-stream-handshake
  type: http
  seq: 9
  tags: [
    manual
  ]
}

get {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/guest/v1/orders/{{order_uid}}/stream?token={{guest_token}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Upgrade: websocket
  Connection: keep-alive, Upgrade
  Sec-WebSocket-Key: dGhlIHNhbXBsZSBub25jZQ==
  Sec-WebSocket-Version: 13
  Origin: http://localhost:3000
}

tests {
  test("the upgrade is accepted for an order this session owns", function () {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(101)
  })
}

docs {
  > **Tagged `manual`. Excluded from automated runs.**
  >
  > A successful upgrade answers `101 Switching Protocols` and leaves the connection upgraded, which
  > silently halts Bruno's HTTP runner -- every request after it is skipped, with no error and a
  > zero exit code. So CI runs `--exclude-tags manual`, and this request is for driving by hand.

  # Diner order stream (handshake only)

Ownership is verified **before** the upgrade: a guest can only subscribe to an order their own
session placed. Without that check, anyone could watch any order by uid, which is exactly the
enumeration the per-order topic scheme exists to prevent.

Try it with another session's token to see the refusal.

## What comes over the socket

Thin events -- an order uid and a status, never a whole order:

    { "type": "order.status_changed", "topic": "order:ord_...", "status": "accepted", "at": "..." }

The client treats each as a **hint to refetch** over HTTP, which is what makes a dropped frame
harmless and why the 5-second polling fallback is a complete substitute rather than a degraded mode
(docs/DECISIONS.md D10).
}
